"1. Vaccine. 2026 May 10;81:128557. doi: 10.1016/j.vaccine.2026.128557. Epub 2026 \nApr 11.\n\nKnowledge, perceptions, and practices of healthcare workers on surveillance of \nadverse events following maternal immunisation in South Africa.\n\nMehta UC(1), Gomba Y(2), Salie I(3), Welte A(4), Pillay N(5), Dahlke M(6), Morof \nD(7), Sekiti V(8), Meyer JC(9).\n\nAuthor information:\n(1)Division of Clinical Pharmacology, University of Cape Town, Cape Town, South \nAfrica. Electronic address: ushma.mehta@uct.ac.za.\n(2)Division of Clinical Pharmacology, University of Cape Town, Cape Town, South \nAfrica.\n(3)Independent Consultant, Cape Town, South Africa.\n(4)School of Nursing and Public Health, College of Health Sciences, University \nof Kwa-Zulu Natal, Durban, South Africa.\n(5)University of the Witwatersrand, Johannesburg, South Africa.\n(6)Global Immunization Division, U.S. Centers of Disease Control and Prevention, \nAtlanta, USA.\n(7)Division of Global HIV and TB, U.S. Centers for Disease Control and \nPrevention, Durban, South Africa.\n(8)Pharmacovigilance Unit, South African Health Products Regulatory Authority, \nPretoria, South Africa.\n(9)Department of Public Health Pharmacy and Management, Sefako Makgatho Health \nSciences University, Pretoria, South Africa; South African Vaccination and \nImmunisation Centre, Sefako Makgatho Health Sciences University, Pretoria, South \nAfrica.\n\nBACKGROUND: As maternal immunisation (MI) programmes expand in South Africa, a \nwell-supported surveillance system for adverse events following immunisation \n(AEFI) designed to include MI is crucial. We assessed the knowledge, \nperceptions, and practices regarding AEFI and MI safety, among public sector \nhealthcare workers (HCWs) involved in maternal and newborn care.\nMETHODS: From July through October 2024, 361 doctors and nurses from 35 public \nfacilities across 7 provinces completed an electronic survey. HCWs from urban, \nperi-urban, and rural settings facilities were included. The survey captured \nsociodemographic characteristics, knowledge of AEFI concepts, reporting \nrequirements, and pregnancy-specific considerations; perceptions of MI safety; \nand AEFI training and reporting practices.\nRESULTS: Overall, 59.7% of participating HCWs (n\u00a0=\u00a0361) demonstrated moderate \nknowledge, yet important gaps were evident. Few correctly recognized that not \nall AEFIs are preventable (25.8%), identified serious adverse events (28.8%), or \ncorrectly identified which vaccines are not recommended during pregnancy \n(21.9%). Many (41.4%) believed pregnant women are more vulnerable to adverse \nevents (AEs) than non-pregnant women. Only 4.4% identified all reportable \npregnancy-related vaccine AEs, and just 15.5% were aware of the Med Safety App, \nthe preferred AEFI reporting tool in South Africa. HCWs expressed positive \nattitudes toward AEFI reporting and confidence in MI safety; uncertainty around \nwhether an AE was vaccine or immunisation-related or not was the main barrier to \nreporting. Nearly one-third (31%) had ever encountered an AEFI, but only 19.7% \never reported one. Facility readiness varied: most had AEFI reporting policies \n(86%) and forms (66%) available, but only 26% maintained a maternal vaccination \nregister.\nCONCLUSION: HCWs support MI and AEFI reporting but demonstrate knowledge gaps in \npregnancy-related vaccine safety and uncertainty about correctly identifying AEs \nthat constitute a reportable AEFMI. Targeted training and capacity-building \ncould improve recognition, documentation, and reporting of AEFMIs, strengthening \nSouth Africa's MI safety monitoring for current and future vaccines.\n\nCopyright \u00a9 2026 The Authors. Published by Elsevier Ltd.. All rights reserved.\n\nDOI: 10.1016/j.vaccine.2026.128557\nPMID: 41967189 [Indexed for MEDLINE]\n\nConflict of interest statement: Declaration of competing interest The authors \ndeclare the following financial interests/personal relationships which may be \nconsidered as potential competing interests:(Ushma C. Mehta reports financial \nsupport was provided by The Task Force for Global Health Inc. The other authors \ndeclare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal \nrelationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this \npaper.)"